Latvian law says nothing about hitchhiking, so on ordinary roads it is legal — and Latvia has no ''automaģistrāle'' category at all, only the ''ātrgaitas ceļš''. [https://likumi.lv/ta/id/274865-celu-satiksmes-noteikumi Paragraph 159.¹ of the ''Ceļu satiksmes noteikumi''], a chapter added in January 2024, forbids the use of an ''ātrgaitas ceļš'' — crossing it included — to pedestrians, to animals outside a vehicle, and to cyclists, e-scooters, mopeds, tractors, carts and anything unable to exceed 60 km/h. Paragraph 159.³ bans stopping and standing on it, together with U-turns and reversing, so no driver can lawfully pull over for you. A forced stop belongs on the ''nomale'' or a designated stopping place under 159.², and that is also where asking makes sense. Everywhere else — the A and P roads that carry most Latvian traffic — hitchhiking is unregulated, so stand on the shoulder and thumb.
